# Results from `tex/s6.tex` concerning the Hopf problem

The main sphere conclusions are unconditional. `Wikipedia.HopfProblem.hopf_problem`
constructs a complex analytic atlas on the literal topological six-sphere, and
`Wikipedia.HopfProblem.hopf_problem_smooth` proves that its underlying real smooth
structure agrees with the sphere's original stereographic atlas. The proof uses
the constructed threefold's homotopy equivalence, `Wikipedia.SmoothSixDPoincare`,
and `Wikipedia.NoExoticSixSphere`; no recognition hypothesis is assumed.
`SixSphereProjection.sphere_projection_holomorphic` proves complex analytic
regularity of the actual transported projection between the literal Euclidean
six- and two-spheres. The map is surjective and null-homotopic. The latter
conclusion uses the unconditional calculation `π₆(S³) ≅ ℤ/12ℤ` from
`Wikipedia.HomotopyGroupsOfSpheres`, together with the constructed global roots.
The older `ConditionalSphereRecognition` module remains a reusable transport
interface, but its hypothesis is not needed by these main results. The supporting
modules prove integral lattice calculations, twisting arithmetic, period-domain
identities, compact complex period tori and their varying holomorphic family,
the exact abstract cyclic group presentation, the exact integral matrix
calculations used in the homology computation, and the Hausdorff toric chart
gluing with its free, properly discontinuous holomorphic twisted lattice action
on a sufficiently small tube. Its Hausdorff complex quotient and holomorphic
covering map are constructed. The quotient is connected and second countable;
its holomorphic projection to the disc is proper and surjective, with compact
connected fibres. Nonzero fibres are parametrized by their constructed
logarithmic period tori via holomorphic embeddings, and are biholomorphic to
them for their ambient-induced fibre atlases. The projection is a submersion
there. The central fibre has centred analytic
normal-crossing charts; its toric branch-count-three stratum consists of exactly
two points, with local equation `t = z₀ z₁ z₂`. Three distinct compact closed
double-curve loci cover its multiple-branch locus and meet pairwise exactly at
these two points. Their analytic atlases and biholomorphisms with the standard
Riemann sphere are constructed; the resulting sphere parametrizations into the
cusp quotient are holomorphic immersions and topological embeddings. The ray
components are compact connected complex surfaces, with holomorphic immersed
inclusions into the toric space. The six charts at the zero ray and the cyclic
hexagonal ray relations are explicit. The component projection `E₀ → W` is a
proper holomorphic immersion with finite fibres of the recorded branch counts,
and is injective on an open dense subset. All ray-subset cones of the fan
meet in exposed common faces. The toric chart and twisted-action Jacobian
identities hold throughout their domains, including the boundary.
The signed volume forms now define the actual holomorphic canonical line
bundles on the toric space and cusp quotient, with nowhere-zero holomorphic
sections and base-preserving, fibrewise linear biholomorphic trivializations.
The toric space and each positive-radius cusp tube are simply connected.
The cusp projection is their actual universal cover, and its fundamental
group is identified with the acting rank-two lattice. The inclusion of
every actual nonzero fibre induces the specified lattice quotient, with
kernel `ker(M₀ - 1)`, not just an abstract presentation of that map.
The genuine first Hurewicz comparison also proves the corresponding
singular homology markings: period-torus `H₁ = ℤ⁴`, cusp `H₁ = ℤ²`, and
the actual inclusion map with that kernel. The generator biholomorphisms
induce the stated integral matrices on actual singular homology.
The actual higher torus homology is now proved in every degree, with
the binomial ranks and integral freeness. The degree-two and degree-three
markings are the genuine exterior powers of the period-loop marking,
with inverses given by actual ordered Pontryagin products. All six
generator actions are the stated ordered-minor matrices. The proof
constructs singular-chain cross products and their normalization,
symmetry, associativity and naturality; no Künneth comparison is assumed.
Actual path lifting in the regular family also constructs fibre transport
and its singular-homology action. In the original period-column marking,
the action agrees with the integral representation, including the inverse
deck-endpoint convention for the specified generator loops.
The explicit section `(t,1,1)` extends over the cusp disc as a holomorphic
closed embedding with the analytic coordinate-axis immersion normal form,
and meets the central fibre at a one-branch point. The canonical volume
is identified with the source's exact logarithmic form on the dense torus.
The section circle is null-homotopic by an explicit based contraction.
The whole punctured cusp, not only each individual fibre, is biholomorphic
over the punctured disc to its explicit logarithmic lattice-orbit quotient.
The source uses the covering atlas of a free holomorphic action on the
contractible logarithmic domain; the cusp retains its inherited open atlas.
For supplied holomorphic cusp expansions, this comparison also identifies
the actual varying period family followed by its clockwise integer-monodromy
quotient with that punctured cusp, with the exact representative and base
formulas. On sufficiently small closed tubes, the explicit straightening
homeomorphism freezes the cusp correction at zero, fixes the central fibre,
and respects both the lattice and compact-fibre-torus actions. The positive
part and its proper polar quotient are constructed. An explicit finite
composition of positive-orthant collapses supplies the positive-part
homotopy. Covering lifts, phase spreading and straightening then give the
actual strong deformation retraction of sufficiently small closed cusp
neighborhoods onto the central fibre, with a nonincreasing base norm.
The central fibre also has its genuine proper phase-quotient description:
the phase fibres are respectively a two-torus, a circle and a point on
its three strata, with the exact collapsed subgroups. Incorporating the
actual positive lattice action and frozen phase gives a homeomorphic
model of the original cusp quotient's central fibre. The prescribed
global honeycomb parameterization is now constructed as a lattice-equivariant
homeomorphism from the real plane onto the actual positive central fibre.
Its hexagons, boundary identifications, and vertex and edge stabilizers
are identified with the actual toric charts. The phase quotient consequently
gives the specified honeycomb model of the central fibre. The controlled
retraction is now also constructed: on each chosen sufficiently small
nonzero fibre its endpoint is exactly the independently defined honeycomb
collapse. It fixes the central fibre, is lattice- and phase-equivariant,
and has nonincreasing base norm. The central fibre's full actual integral
homology is free with ranks `(1,2,4,2,1)`, vanishes above degree four,
and has Euler characteristic two. Its actual fundamental group is `ℤ²`.
The prescribed collapse induces the actual marked surjection in degree
one, with integral kernel `im(M₀ - 1) = ker(M₀ - 1)`. One chosen retraction
restricts, on every other sufficiently small nonzero level, to a map
homotopic to that level's independently prescribed collapse; consequently
their actual induced homology maps agree in every degree. The complete
higher-degree specialization matrix calculation remains separate.

The actual compact component `E₀` has a proper holomorphic blow-down to the
actual complex projective plane. The inverse images of its three affine
patches are biholomorphic to the incidence-model blow-up of `ℂ²` at zero,
with exactly the standard blow-down maps. Thus `E₀` is the blow-up at the
three independent coordinate points, the three-point model of `dP₆`.
Its three exceptional fibres are the odd hexagon boundary curves, parametrized
by holomorphic closed Riemann-sphere embeddings and immersions. Away from
these curves, blow-down is biholomorphic to the projective plane minus the
